course: dictionary
lesson: 11121
title: Who, whom and whose
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Who» is a small word that stands in for a person. You use it to ask about a person: «Who is at the door?» Here «who» asks for a person, not a number, a place or a thing.
explain: «Who» can also join two ideas about one person: «The girl who won is my friend.» The words «who won» tell you more about the girl, so «who» links the two parts of the sentence together.
explain: «Whom» is the same word used when someone receives something or something is done to them: «to whom». «Whose» shows who something belongs to: «Whose bag is this?» Adults often use «whom» in letters, so it is good to know it.
fact: «Who» is a pronoun, a word that stands in for a person's name.
fact: «Who» asks about a person, as in «Who is at the door?»
fact: «Who» tells more about a person just named, as in «The girl who won is my friend».
fact: «Whom» is used when something is done to the person: «to whom».
fact: «Whose» asks or tells who something belongs to. In casual talk, «who cares?» means it does not matter, and «who knows?» means nobody knows.
exercise: What does «who» ask about in «Who is at the door?» | *A person | A colour | A time | A number
exercise: In «The girl who won is my friend», what do the words «who won» do? | Asks a question | *Tell more about the girl | Show whose it is | Mean it does not matter
exercise: What does «whose» ask in «Whose bag is this?» | How many bags there are | *Who something belongs to | Which person is at the door | A place where something is kept
test: In casual talk, «Who cares?» means what? | Nobody can see the answer | *It does not matter | Please tell me your name | Everybody is coming
test: «Who asked you?» is a rude put-down. Which word is the rude put-down? | *A rude way to tell someone to keep out of things | The start of a formal letter | A greeting you say when you meet a friend | A question about a person's age
exam: Which word completes the formal phrase «to ___ it may concern»? | which | who | *whom | whose
